<h2 id="trace-section-L-function" class="tex-section"><span data-tag="03UU">64.19</span> L-functions</h2>

<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="03UV">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UV">Definition <span data-tag="03UV">64.19.1</span>.</a> Let $X$ be a scheme of finite type over a finite field $k$. Let $\Lambda $ be a finite ring of order prime to the characteristic of $k$ and $\mathcal{F}$ a constructible flat $\Lambda $-module on $X_{\acute{e}tale}$. Then we set </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  L(X, \mathcal{F}) := \prod \nolimits _{x\in |X|} \det (1 - \pi _ x^*T^{\deg x} |_{\mathcal{F}_{\bar x}})^{-1} \in \Lambda [[ T ]]  \]
</div>
  <p> where $|X|$ is the set of closed points of $X$, $\deg x = [\kappa (x): k]$ and $\bar x$ is a geometric point lying over $x$. This definition clearly generalizes to the case where $\mathcal{F}$ is replaced by a $K \in D_{ctf}(X, \Lambda )$. We call this the <em>$L$-function of $\mathcal{F}$</em>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-remark" id="03UW">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UW">Remark <span data-tag="03UW">64.19.2</span>.</a> Intuitively, $T$ should be thought of as $T = t^ f$ where $p^ f = \#  k$. The definitions are then independent of the size of the ground field. </p>
</article> 
</p>
<p>
<article class="env-definition" id="03UX">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/03UX">Definition <span data-tag="03UX">64.19.3</span>.</a> Now assume that $\mathcal{F}$ is a $\mathbf{Q}_\ell $-sheaf on $X$. In this case we define </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  L(X, \mathcal{F}) := \prod \nolimits _{x \in |X|} \det (1 - \pi _ x^*T^{\deg x} |_{\mathcal{F}_{\bar x}})^{-1} \in \mathbf{Q}_\ell [[T]].  \]
</div>
  <p> Note that this product converges since there are finitely many points of a given degree. We call this the <em>$L$-function of $\mathcal{F}$</em>. </p>
</article> 
</p>
